<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry the Blind</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1111–1196</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Henry IV of Luxembourg, Henry I of Namur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry the Blind (c. 1111–1196) was Count of Namur and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, ruling a large group of lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> valley. Long without an heir, he promised his inheritance to his nephew Baldwin V of Hainaut, but the late birth of his daughter Ermesinde in 1186 led to a dispute: Namur went to Hainaut and Luxembourg eventually to Ermesinde.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-the-blind.jpg" alt="Henry the Blind" width="243" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Augustin Calmet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_IV,_Count_of_Luxembourg.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry was the second known son of Count Godfrey I of Namur and Ermesinde, a daughter of Count Conrad I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>. He first appears in the record in the foundation charter of the abbey of Floreffe in 1121, and after his elder brother Albert died young, about 1127, he was associated with the government of Namur from 1131. When Conrad II of Luxembourg died in 1136, the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a> granted Henry the county and its advocacies, among them those of the abbeys of Saint-Maximin at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and Echternach, keeping them from the French count of Grandpré.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Henry inherited Namur on his father's death in 1139 and gathered further lordships in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a>: Durbuy by 1151 and La Roche-en-Ardenne by early 1153, with the advocacy of the abbey of Stavelot-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malmedy">Malmedy</a>, while Longwy came from his mother. After a battle at Fosses-la-Ville in 1140 he made peace with Bishop Adalbero II of Liège in 1141 and helped lead the bishop's successful expedition against Bouillon. He married Laurette, daughter of Count Thierry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, between 1152 and 1159, but they separated in 1163.</p><p>Being without a child, he named as heir his brother-in-law Count Baldwin IV of Hainault, and after Baldwin's death in 1171 Baldwin's son. In 1171 he married Agnes, daughter of Count Henry I of Guelders, but sent her back to her father two years later. He lost his sight in 1182 after a serious illness, rejoined his wife in 1184, and the birth of their daughter led him to treat his promise to Baldwin as void.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Under the settlement reached, Baldwin was to have Namur and Ermesinde Durbuy and La Roche, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> were handed out in 1189, when Henry abdicated in Namur. He betrothed Ermesinde first to Henry II of Champagne and then to Theobald I of Bar. Defeated by Henry of Limburg at Noville-sur-Mehaigne on 1 August 1194, he died at Echternach two years later. He was the last of the first line of counts of Namur and the most powerful of them; the byname the Blind came only much later.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry the Blind” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Count <b>Henry the Blind</b> also held Luxembourg in the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-namur">County of Namur</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Echternach, in what is now <a href="/countries/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.81,6.42&amp;year=1166" aria-label="Echternach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Henry the Blind"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5354 5850h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5354 5850h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=49.81,6.42&amp;year=1166">What was here in 1166? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
